  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe address the difficulty of finding the Erd\u0151s constant C by employing a gradient-based method with custom repair modules designed to adjust solution boundaries. The primary bottleneck in the previous implementation is the potential for local optima trapping. Our new approach uses a systematic gradient evaluation and perturbation schedule with adaptive repair mechanisms, ensuring solution feasibility throughout the search. This method is distinct because it decouples gradient computation from target shifting, using a corruption schedule that reparameterizes the shifting function to handle discontinuities more effectively.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe objective remains: minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx subject to \u2211h_i=n/2 and 0\u2264h_i\u22641.\n\nRepresentation: h as a vector p \u2208 {0,1}^n with sum fixed. The alternative uses real-valued vector r with the transformation r_i = 0/1 boundaries enforced via repair mechanisms after affine projection. The gradient is computed via:\ncorr = np.correlate(p, 1-p, mode='full')\nworst_shift = np.argmax(corr) - (n-1)\nThen gradient g_i = \n  if shift exists: \n      if i < n-shifts: term = (1-r[i+shift]) or (-r[i-shift] if negative shift)\n  else: whole array normalized penalty\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Start at parent solution if available (n=192)\n- While time not exceeded:\n  1. Compute worst gradient using explicit shift-aware calculation\n  2. Apply guided perturbation selecting indices based on gradient sign\n  3. Repair solution if out-of-bound values or sum deviation\n  4. Evaluate new candidate and accept if improvement threshold (0.001) met\n\n4. Search choices\nPopulation rule: Use a repair cycle with maximum 1500 iterations per repair\nTemperature schedule: Maintain step scale between 0.01-0.8 down-adaptive to progress rate\n\n5. Compute budget\nEstimate evaluation time: ~0.2 seconds per candidate at n=192\nMax candidates: 3000 evaluations (allowing ~600 seconds)\nRepair time factor: ~6 times faster than the reference approach\n\n6. Validation contract\nEnsure final solution satisfies:\n  - All values \u2208 [0,1]\n  - Sum equals n/2 exactly\n  - Lower C-bound observed than parent (0.380879)\nAnd return format: [h.tolist(), float(c5), int(n)]",
